 succeeded in 0ms:
    80	  loop) + the user-facing `quantbook` Python package (`register_formula_function`, `qb.*`). The
    81	  Rust `src/lib.rs` may host a PyO3 bridge later; for v1 the worker is a **plain Python process**
    82	  speaking the Arrow-IPC protocol over stdio — no PyO3 in the hot path (simpler, debugpy-friendly,
    83	  no GIL-in-Rust complications). Promote `quantbook-py` to a workspace member.
    84	- **`FunctionImplHandle(u64)`** indexes a worker-side registry of user functions (the handle is
    85	  minted IDE-side when the user calls `register_formula_function`, passed to `registerFunction`
    86	  over napi, stored in `udf_handles`, and echoed in each dispatch request so the worker knows which
    87	  Python callable to invoke).
    88	
    89	## 3. Wire protocol (length-prefixed Arrow IPC over a duplex pipe)
    90	
    91	Framing: `[u32 LE length][u8 frame-type][payload]`. Frame types:
    92	- `HELLO` / `HELLO_ACK` — handshake: protocol version, worker pid (for debugpy), capability flags.
    93	- `CALL { handle: u64, call_id: u64, args: Arrow RecordBatch }` — one UDF invocation. Args encoded
    94	  as an Arrow batch (scalars = 1-row; ranges/columns = N-row; the `ArgContext`/`BatchShape`
    95	  metadata determines the schema). `call_id` correlates the response + supports cancel.
    96	- `RETURN { call_id, result: Arrow RecordBatch }` — success; maps to `FunctionReturn::Scalar`
    97	  (1×1) or `Array` (N×M).
    98	- `RAISE { call_id, exc_type, message, traceback }` — Python raised; maps to a deterministic
    99	  `CellDiagnostic` + the cell value `#CALC!`/`#VALUE!` per contract (exit test 7).
   100	- `CANCEL { call_id }` — cooperative cancel request (best-effort; hard cancel is process kill).
   101	- `LOG { level, message }` — worker stdout/stderr surfaced as diagnostics (never silently dropped).
   102	
   103	**Arrow ↔ engine `Value` mapping** (the fiddly part — its own cycle):
   104	- engine `Value::{Number, Text, Boolean, Error, Blank}` ↔ Arrow `Float64 / Utf8 / Boolean / (error
   105	  as a sentinel union or a separate error column) / null`. `ArrayValue` ↔ a 2-D batch (row-major).
   106	  Error values need an explicit encoding (Arrow has no native spreadsheet-error type) — a tagged
   107	  struct column `{ kind: Utf8 ("number"|"text"|"bool"|"error"|"blank"), num, str, bool, err }` is
   108	  the safe v1 encoding (verbose but unambiguous; optimize later).
   109	
   110	## 4. Eval-path wiring (`RegisteredFn::Udf`)
   111	
   112	1. Add `RegisteredFn::Udf(FunctionImplHandle)` to the enum (`registry.rs:172`).
   113	2. `register_udf` (6.4-2) currently stores metadata + handle but registers NO `RegisteredFn`.
   114	   6.4-3: also insert `RegisteredFn::Udf(handle)` into the dispatch table (`fns`) so the
   115	   `scalar.rs` match finds it instead of falling to the `None` #NAME? arm. (Re-examine the
   116	   6.4-2 atomicity invariant — `register_udf` becomes a 3-way atomic: metadata + handle + dispatch.)
   117	3. New match arm in `scalar.rs` (~before `:463`): `Some(RegisteredFn::Udf(handle)) => { … }` —
   118	   marshal the evaluated args to an Arrow batch, call `ctx.worker.call(handle, batch, deadline)`,
   119	   map `RETURN`→`FunctionReturn`, `RAISE`→error value + diagnostic, timeout→worker-kill + `#CALC!`.
   120	4. The `FunctionContext` / `EvalContext` gains access to the `WorkerHandle` (a new optional field;
   121	   `None` when no worker is configured → registered UDF with no worker = deterministic
   122	   `#CALC!`/diagnostic, NOT a panic — No-Fallbacks-honest).
   123	
   124	## 5. Cancellation / worker-kill (contract §6.2 — hard cancel)
   125	
   126	- `CancelPolicy::WorkerKill` (already a `FunctionMetadata` axis, 6.4-1) → cancel/timeout kills the
   127	  worker process. The engine must survive a killed worker: the `WorkerHandle` respawns lazily on
   128	  the next call; in-flight `call_id`s resolve to a deterministic cancel diagnostic.
   129	- Exit test 6: a canceled/timed-out UDF must NOT commit a late result — `call_id` correlation
   130	  drops any `RETURN` arriving after the deadline/kill.
   131	- FaultGuard discipline (6.1C M3 / 6.4-2): a worker round-trip happening inside recompute must not,
   132	  on worker death, leave the session torn. The worker call is I/O at the leaf; failures map to a
   133	  cell error, not an engine fault.
   134	
   135	## 6. debugpy
   136	
   137	- Worker spawned with an env/flag that, in trusted-workspace mode, starts `debugpy` listening on a
   138	  port (surfaced in `HELLO_ACK` → IDE can attach). Off by default; opt-in per the trusted-workspace
   139	  gate. No effect on the protocol hot path.
   140	

