codex
VERDICT: DO-NOT-SHIP

ID: timeout-kill-can-deadlock-on-inherited-stdout  
SEVERITY: HIGH  
LOCATION: `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:108-119`, `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:355-358`  
WHAT: `WorkerProcess::drop` kills only the direct Python child, waits it, then unconditionally joins the reader thread assuming stdout EOF. A UDF can spawn a subprocess/grandchild that inherits fd 1; after the Python child is killed, that grandchild keeps the pipe write-end open, so the reader blocks forever in `read_frame` and `join()` never returns.  
WHY: A timeout/cancel path can hang the engine permanently instead of returning `UdfError::Timeout`, violating the hard-cancel contract and zombie/lifecycle requirements.  
FIX: Spawn the worker in its own process group/session and kill the whole group on timeout/drop. Also make reader shutdown bounded or nonblocking; do not rely on direct-child death implying stdout EOF.

ID: udf-print-corrupts-protocol-stdout  
SEVERITY: HIGH  
LOCATION: `crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/worker.py:30-55`, `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:164-166`  
WHAT: The protocol uses `sys.stdout.buffer`, but user module import and UDF execution run with normal `sys.stdout` still pointing at the protocol fd. Any `print()` or library stdout write during import or call injects raw bytes into the frame stream.  
WHY: A common UDF behavior desynchronizes the worker protocol. It is not converted to `LOG`, despite design §3 requiring stdout/stderr diagnostics not be silently dropped.  
FIX: Reserve fd 1 exclusively for protocol writes. Before importing user code, replace `sys.stdout` with a stderr/log proxy, or capture stdout/stderr and emit `LOG` frames through the protocol writer.

ID: call-deadline-excludes-respawn-handshake-and-write  
SEVERITY: MED  
LOCATION: `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:241`, `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:287-308`  
WHAT: `call()` performs `ensure_started()` and writes the CALL before starting `deadline_at`. A short-deadline call that must respawn can block up to `handshake_timeout + write time + deadline`.  
WHY: `UdfWorker::call` is documented as subject to `deadline`; this violates deadline accounting, especially after a killed worker or hung import.  
FIX: Compute an absolute deadline at call entry. Pass remaining time into spawn/handshake, cap handshake timeout by remaining, and cover CALL encode/write/flush.

ID: log-or-stale-frame-flood-can-defeat-timeout  
SEVERITY: HIGH  
LOCATION: `crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:307-342`  
WHAT: The loop uses `recv_timeout(remaining)`, but if queued `LOG` or stale-call frames are always available, it keeps processing and `continue`s even after `remaining` reaches zero.  
WHY: A buggy/hostile worker can keep the recalc thread alive indefinitely with nonterminal frames, so the deadline never triggers. The unbounded `mpsc` channel can also grow without bound.  
FIX: Check `Instant::now() >= deadline_at` before processing another nonterminal frame, bound/log-coalesce `LOG` handling, and use a bounded channel or backpressure policy.

ID: registry-silently-overwrites-invalid-handles  
SEVERITY: MED  
LOCATION: `crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/__init__.py:18-27`, `crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/_registry.py:8-13`  
WHAT: `register_formula_function` coerces `handle` with `int()` and blindly stores it. Duplicate handles overwrite prior functions; negative or >u64 handles are accepted; non-callables are accepted.  
WHY: This can route a shipped handle to the wrong callable or make registration appear successful when the engine can never call it, violating No-Fallbacks/error visibility.  
FIX: Validate `callable(fn)`, require `0 <= handle <= u64::MAX`, and reject duplicate registration unless an explicit replace flag is provided.

CLEAN AREAS: Rust/Python frame tags, length prefixing, CALL/RETURN headers, HELLO/HELLO_ACK, and RAISE byte layouts match. Rust decode paths avoid panics on malformed worker-controlled frames in the reviewed codec/control/frame code. Direct-child handshake failure paths do get reaped, except for the inherited-stdout/process-tree hole above.
